// "Why it works" for Exam 3: the basic science behind lines in the Exam 3 notes, from the other textbooks
// (OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e, Chemistry 2e, Microbiology, Psychology 2e, Introduction to Sociology 3e and
// Fundamentals of Nursing, all CC BY 4.0, and Open RN Nursing Pharmacology 2e, CC BY 4.0). Nothing here is new exam
// content: each entry quotes a line already in that topic's notes (says, an exact phrase of the notes, checked by
// tools/build_e3_notes.js) and explains the reason behind it (why). src names the book and section.
window.BASICS = {
'53-overview': [
  { says: 'a hormone acts only where a receptor fits it (lock and key)', why: 'Hormones reach every tissue in the blood, but a cell can only answer one it has a receptor for. Water-soluble hormones (insulin, glucagon, TSH) can\'t cross the fatty cell membrane, so they bind a receptor on the surface and a "second messenger" such as cAMP carries the order inside. Lipid-soluble hormones (cortisol and the other steroids, and thyroid hormone) pass into the cell and act on receptors that switch genes on — so their effects build more slowly and last longer. Cells can also add receptors when a hormone is scarce (upregulation) and remove them when it is in excess (downregulation).', src: 'A&P 2e 17.2' },
  { says: 'negative feedback turns secretion off once the need is met', why: 'Negative feedback keeps a hormone in a narrow range: when the level is high enough, the gland (or the hypothalamus and pituitary above it) stops sending the signal. The textbook example is cortisol: as glucocorticoid levels rise, the hypothalamus and pituitary cut back the signal to the adrenal glands. The same loop explains why a medicine that supplies a hormone (prednisone, levothyroxine) quiets the body\'s own production of it.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.2' },
  { says: 'Many hormones are released in pulses, so one sample can mislead', why: 'Hormones are cleared from the blood quickly. A hormone\'s half-life is the time for half of it to be broken down: about 60 to 90 minutes for cortisol, which travels on a carrier protein, and only about one minute for epinephrine. With levels rising and falling that fast, one blood draw is a snapshot of a single moment, while a 24-hour collection adds up the whole day.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.2' }
],
'53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol': [
  { says: 'the kidney reabsorbs sodium and water and excretes potassium', why: 'In the distal tubules and collecting ducts, aldosterone switches on sodium-potassium pumps. Sodium is pulled from the filtrate back into the blood and water follows it by osmosis, so blood volume and pressure rise; potassium goes the other way, into the urine. That single exchange explains the findings at both extremes: too much aldosterone means fluid retention and low potassium, too little means volume loss and high potassium.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.3, 17.6' },
  { says: 'antiinflammatory (fewer inflammatory cells reach injuries)', why: 'One of cortisol\'s jobs is to turn the immune system down, which damps the inflammatory response. That is exactly what glucocorticoid medicines are used for — cortisone shots into inflamed joints, prednisone tablets, hydrocortisone cream for itchy rashes. The flip side is the one nurses watch for: sustained high cortisol, from illness, stress or a steroid drug, weakens immunity and makes infection more likely.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.6 · Psychology 2e 14.1' },
  { says: 'drive the fight-or-flight stress response', why: 'In danger, the hypothalamus fires the adrenal medulla through sympathetic nerves and epinephrine and norepinephrine flood the blood within seconds: heart rate and blood pressure rise, airways open, the liver turns glycogen into glucose, and blood is shunted away from the gut, kidneys and skin. Selye called this the alarm reaction. If the stress continues, the slower HPA axis takes over with cortisol — and months of it lead to exhaustion: fatigue, depression and weakened immunity.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.6 · Psychology 2e 14.1' }
],
'53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre': [
  { says: 'Both are made in the hypothalamus and only stored here', why: 'ADH is produced by neurons in the hypothalamus and delivered to the posterior pituitary for storage and release. Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus sense when the blood becomes too concentrated and trigger ADH release; ADH makes the kidney reabsorb more water, diluting the blood back to normal. That is why ADH problems show up as thirst, urine volume and sodium changes.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.3, 26.2' },
  { says: 'Most circulating hormone is bound to plasma proteins', why: 'Less than 1% of circulating T3 and T4 is free; about 99% rides on thyroxine-binding globulin, albumin and other plasma proteins. Only the free hormone can cross cell membranes and act, so the bound pool is a reserve that is released as free levels fall. Many cells then remove one iodine from T4 to make T3, the more potent form — which is why the free levels are the ones that matter.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' },
  { says: 'Iodide absorbed from the gut is trapped and concentrated by the thyroid', why: 'When TSH binds the follicle cells, they actively pump iodide in from the blood until it is many times more concentrated than in the bloodstream. Peroxidase enzymes attach the iodine to tyrosine on thyroglobulin, making T4 (four iodines) and T3 (three), which are stored in the colloid until TSH releases them. Because the gland grabs iodine this way, a dose of radioactive iodine goes almost straight to the thyroid.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' },
  { says: 'PTH acts on three targets', why: 'When blood calcium drops, the chief cells release PTH. In bone it activates osteoclasts, which dissolve bone matrix and free calcium; in the kidney it saves calcium (and magnesium) and dumps phosphate; and it starts the kidney\'s production of calcitriol, the active vitamin D the intestine needs to absorb dietary calcium. As calcium rises, PTH release is switched off — negative feedback.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.5, 26.3' },
  { says: 'Insulin lowers glucose by moving it into cells', why: 'Insulin binds its receptor and sends vesicles full of glucose transporters to the cell surface, so glucose can enter by facilitated diffusion. Skeletal muscle and fat cells are its main targets. Brain cells, red blood cells, liver, kidney and the lining of the small intestine don\'t need insulin to take up glucose. Insulin also tells the liver to store glucose as glycogen and to stop making new glucose.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.9' }
],
'53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g': [
  { says: 'Less ADH — urine stays dilute', why: 'Water balance depends on two signals: osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus trigger ADH (so the kidney keeps water) and the thirst response (so we drink). Some older adults develop a decreased sense of thirst, so the drink signal is weak at the same time the kidney is less able to concentrate urine. Neither safeguard works well, which is why scheduled fluids — not waiting for thirst — protect older patients from dehydration.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.2 · Fundamentals of Nursing 41.3' },
  { says: 'Less glucose tolerance', why: 'With age, blood glucose spikes faster after eating and takes longer to come back down, because cells slowly become less sensitive to insulin. Almost 27% of Americans aged 65 and older have diabetes. That is why the textbook asks about persistent thirst, slow-healing wounds and repeated yeast infections in older adults: they can be the first clues.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.11' },
  { says: 'Slower general metabolism — less cold tolerance', why: 'The aging thyroid makes less hormone, so the basal metabolic rate falls gradually. Thyroid hormone is what makes cells burn fuel and release heat, so a lower rate means less body heat and more body fat. These normal changes look like mild hypothyroidism, which is why the notes compare the patient to their own baseline before calling it disease.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.11, 17.4' }
],
'53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica': [
  { says: 'darkened skin over joints and scars with some adrenal disorders (high ACTH and MSH)', why: 'ACTH is cut from a larger precursor molecule, pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC), which also yields melanocyte-stimulating hormone. When the adrenal cortex fails, low cortisol removes the negative feedback, the pituitary makes much more POMC, and the extra melanocyte stimulation darkens the skin. Hyperpigmentation is listed among the signs of autoimmune Addison disease, along with weakness, low sodium, high potassium and low glucose.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.3 · Microbiology 19.2' },
  { says: 'type 2 diabetes is more common in some racial and ethnic minority groups and in people of lower socioeconomic status', why: 'Sociology explains this as a disparity in health, not biology alone: African American, Mexican American and Native American people have higher rates of diabetes and related deaths, and even after adjusting for insurance, minority groups receive poorer-quality care and less access to care. Race and socioeconomic status are intertwined, since minority groups are more likely to be poor — so the same patient often faces both barriers.', src: 'Sociology 3e 19.3' }
],
'53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job': [
  { says: 'Salivary levels of steroid hormones', why: 'Steroid hormones are made from cholesterol, so they slip through lipid cell membranes — including the cells of the salivary glands — and the free hormone in blood shows up in saliva. Protein and peptide hormones are water-soluble and can\'t cross a lipid membrane; they act through receptors on the cell surface instead. That chemistry is why cortisol can be tested in saliva but pituitary hormones cannot.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.2' },
  { says: 'catecholamines go on ice to the lab immediately', why: 'Epinephrine has a half-life of about one minute in the body — catecholamines are broken down very quickly. A sample left at room temperature keeps losing them, and the result comes back falsely low. Keeping the tube cold and getting it to the lab fast preserves what was actually in the blood when it was drawn.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.2' },
  { says: 'the patient swallows a pill of radioactive iodine, which collects in the thyroid', why: 'The thyroid actively traps iodide to build its hormones, and it can\'t tell a radioactive iodine atom from a normal one. Iodine-131 concentrates in the thyroid, so the gamma rays coming from the neck show how much tissue is active and where. The same uptake, with a larger dose, is how radioactive iodine treats Graves disease.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4 · Chemistry 2e 21.5' }
],
'55-overview': [
  { says: 'when the thyroid makes too little hormone, the pituitary keeps raising TSH, and TSH makes thyroid cells grow', why: 'Low T3 and T4 remove the negative feedback, so the hypothalamus (TRH) and pituitary (TSH) keep calling for more. In iodine deficiency, for example, TSH climbs, thyroglobulin builds up in the follicles and the gland swells into a goiter — without making more hormone. In Graves disease antibodies hit the same TSH receptor, so the gland also grows. A goiter shows the gland is being stimulated, not which way the hormone level is going.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4 · Microbiology 19.2' },
  { says: 'Low calcium makes nerves and muscles too excitable', why: 'Calcium normally steadies cell membranes. When blood calcium falls, membranes become more permeable to sodium, so nerves and muscles fire too easily: twitching, cramping, spasms and convulsions, and severe deficits can paralyze the breathing muscles. High calcium does the reverse — less sodium permeability and a sluggish nervous system. This is why hypocalcemia after neck surgery is an emergency.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.5, 26.3' },
  { says: 'Calcium and phosphorus move in opposite directions', why: 'Over 90% of the body\'s calcium and phosphate sits in bone as calcium-phosphate salts. PTH dissolves those salts to raise blood calcium, but in the kidney it saves calcium while making the body lose phosphate. So when PTH is high, calcium goes up and phosphate goes down; when PTH is missing, calcium falls and phosphate builds up.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.3, 17.5' }
],
'55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e': [
  { says: 'TSH high in primary hypothyroidism (the gland fails, the pituitary shouts)', why: 'Thyroid hormone is controlled by a classic negative feedback loop: low T3 and T4 trigger TRH from the hypothalamus and TSH from the pituitary, and rising T3 and T4 turn TSH down. When the gland itself fails, the loop is intact but unanswered, so TSH climbs. If the pituitary or hypothalamus is the problem (secondary disease), TSH is low or normal even though the hormone is low.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' },
  { says: 'cold intolerance (extra blankets or a sweater in warm weather)', why: 'T3 and T4 act on receptors in the mitochondria and speed up the breakdown of nutrients and the use of oxygen to make ATP. The process is inefficient, so a lot of energy escapes as heat — the calorigenic (heat-making) effect. With too little thyroid hormone the cells burn less fuel and make less heat: cold hands, low temperature and weight gain all come from the same slowdown.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' },
  { says: 'Keep iron, calcium and antacids at least 4 hours apart', why: 'A pill swallowed by mouth is absorbed only as well as conditions in the gut allow. Open RN lists the variables: the acidity of the stomach contents, how fast the stomach empties, what food is present, and other drugs taken at the same time. Minerals and antacids interfere with levothyroxine absorption, and a dose that is absorbed differently from day to day can\'t be adjusted accurately — which is why the timing rule matters.', src: 'Nursing Pharmacology 2e 1.3' }
],
'55-myxedema-coma': [
  { says: 'IV T3 and/or T4', why: 'Absorption from the GI tract depends on blood flow to the gut and on how fast the stomach empties. In myxedema coma metabolism, circulation and gut motility are all profoundly slowed, so a pill would be absorbed late and unpredictably. An IV dose is fully available at once — the reason replacement in this emergency goes into the vein.', src: 'Nursing Pharmacology 2e 1.3' }
],
'55-hyperthyroidism-and-graves-disease': [
  { says: 'antibodies called thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSIs) bind the TSH receptor and drive the gland', why: 'Graves disease is an organ-specific autoimmune disease. The immune system makes an antibody (TSI, also called TSH-receptor antibody) that fits the TSH receptor like a key and switches it on — the gland is stimulated nonstop no matter what the pituitary says. The result is heat intolerance, a fast or irregular heartbeat, weight loss, goiter and the bulging eyes of Graves ophthalmopathy.', src: 'Microbiology 19.2' },
  { says: 'Heat intolerance is often the first symptom', why: 'Thyroid hormone makes cells burn more fuel and use more oxygen, and because the process is inefficient, much of that energy comes off as heat — the calorigenic effect. With too much hormone, the furnace is turned up all day: excessive body heat, sweating, a craving for cool rooms and weight loss despite a big appetite.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' },
  { says: 'higher systolic and lower diastolic BP (wide pulse pressure), atrial fibrillation', why: 'Thyroid hormone increases the body\'s sensitivity to epinephrine and norepinephrine by adding more of their receptors (upregulation). With excess hormone, the same normal adrenaline now speeds the heart, strengthens each beat and raises pressure. This is also why a beta blocker relieves the racing heart and tremor even though it does nothing to the hormone level.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' },
  { says: 'short half-life — no radiation precautions, but rule out pregnancy', why: 'A radioactive isotope\'s half-life is the time for half of it to decay. The iodine used for the uptake scan decays away quickly, so very little radiation is left to protect others from. Radiation damages DNA and its effects are strongest in rapidly dividing cells such as an embryo\'s, which is why pregnancy is ruled out first.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 21.3, 21.6' }
],
'55-treating-hyperthyroidism-drugs-radioacti': [
  { says: 'They block hormone production by stopping iodide binding in the gland', why: 'Making thyroid hormone takes several steps: the follicle cells trap iodide, oxidize it, and peroxidase enzymes attach the iodine to tyrosine on thyroglobulin. The thionamides interfere with that binding step, so no new hormone is assembled. But finished T3 and T4 are already stored in the colloid and keep being released when TSH calls for them — which is why relief from methimazole or PTU takes weeks.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' },
  { says: 'The thyroid takes it up and the local radiation destroys some hormone-producing cells', why: 'Iodine-131 concentrates in the thyroid gland, which is why it is used to treat Graves disease. Its radiation damages the DNA of the cells that trapped it, so the overactive tissue is destroyed from the inside while the rest of the body gets little exposure. I-131 has a half-life of about 8 days, so the effect builds over weeks and stored hormone keeps symptoms going meanwhile.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 21.5, 21.3, 21.6' },
  { says: 'Stay at least 6 feet from pregnant women, infants and young children', why: 'The three rules for reducing radiation exposure are shielding, distance and time: stand farther from the source and limit how long you are near it. The patient is the source for a while, and the people kept farthest away are the ones whose cells divide fastest — a fetus, infants and young children — because radiation damage is greatest in rapidly reproducing cells.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 21.6' },
  { says: 'Beta blockers — atenolol or propranolol relieve sweating, anxiety, tachycardia and palpitations — supportive only', why: 'Excess thyroid hormone upregulates the receptors for epinephrine and norepinephrine, so the heart and vessels overreact to normal adrenaline. A beta blocker sits on those receptors and blunts the response — the heart slows and the tremor eases — but it doesn\'t touch the gland, so hormone production goes on until the thionamide, iodine or radioactive iodine works.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4 · Nursing Pharmacology 2e 4.2' }
],
'55-thyroidectomy-and-thyroid-storm': [
  { says: 'if the parathyroids are removed, bruised or lose blood supply, PTH falls → hypocalcemia', why: 'The parathyroid glands are tiny and embedded in the back of the thyroid, so they are easily injured or removed with it — the A&P text names thyroid surgery as a cause of hypoparathyroidism. Without PTH, calcium falls; low calcium makes nerve membranes leak sodium, causing tingling, twitching and spasms, and severe deficits can paralyze breathing muscles. That is why calcium gluconate stays at the bedside.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.5, 26.3' },
  { says: 'Bleeding can show as respiratory distress from tracheal compression', why: 'The thyroid wraps around the front of the trachea just below the larynx. Blood collecting in the closed space of the neck has nowhere to go but inward, pressing on the airway. That is why the nurse looks behind the neck, where blood pools, and treats new breathing trouble after thyroid surgery as bleeding until proven otherwise.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' },
  { says: 'aspirin displaces thyroid hormone from its carrier proteins', why: 'About 99% of circulating T3 and T4 is bound to carrier proteins and only the free fraction can enter cells and act. Anything that pushes hormone off its carriers instantly raises the active, free hormone — the last thing a patient in thyroid storm needs. Acetaminophen lowers the fever without that effect.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.4' }
],
'55-thyroiditis-and-thyroid-cancer': [
  { says: 'antibodies and lymphocytes destroy thyroid tissue → hypothyroidism', why: 'Hashimoto thyroiditis is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the United States. It is a cell-mediated autoimmune disease: cytotoxic lymphocytes, macrophages and autoantibodies attack the gland, causing goiter, cold intolerance, weakness, depression and memory loss. People with Hashimoto are also more likely to develop other autoimmune diseases — Addison disease, type 1 diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis and celiac disease.', src: 'Microbiology 19.2' }
],
'55-hypoparathyroidism-and-hyperparathyroidi': [
  { says: 'In bone, PTH slows osteoblasts and speeds osteoclasts', why: 'Bone is the body\'s calcium bank. Osteoclasts withdraw — they secrete enzymes that dissolve bone matrix and release calcium; osteoblasts deposit. PTH stimulates the osteoclasts and inhibits the osteoblasts, so with too much PTH, calcium keeps leaving bone: density falls and bones fracture or deform, while the extra calcium deposits in tissues and organs.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.5' },
  { says: 'serum calcium, magnesium, PTH and albumin; then a 24-hour urine calcium', why: 'A little more than half of blood calcium is bound to proteins, mainly albumin, so a low albumin can make total calcium look low when the active (ionized) calcium is fine — albumin is checked to read calcium correctly. Calcium and phosphate output in urine varies a lot over the day, which is why they are measured in a 24-hour collection instead of a single sample.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.3' },
  { says: 'low enough to keep urine calcium down and prevent kidney stones', why: 'Normally PTH makes the kidney tubules reabsorb calcium from the filtrate. Without PTH, more of the filtered calcium is lost in the urine. Raise blood calcium too far with supplements and the kidney spills even more into the urine, where it can form stones — so the target is the low-normal range: enough to stop the tingling, not enough to load the urine.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.5, 26.3' },
  { says: 'cinacalcet (a calcimimetic) binds calcium-sensing receptors on the parathyroids', why: 'PTH is under negative feedback: rising blood calcium inhibits further PTH release. A calcimimetic imitates calcium at that sensor, so the gland "sees" more calcium than is really there and cuts PTH output — and blood calcium falls.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.5' }
],
'56-overview': [
  { says: 'It binds membrane receptors and opens cells to glucose', why: 'Insulin activates a receptor that sends vesicles full of glucose transporters to the cell membrane, where glucose enters by facilitated diffusion. Muscle and fat cells depend on this, but brain cells, red blood cells, liver, kidney and the intestinal lining do not need insulin to take up glucose. So when insulin is missing, glucose piles up in the blood while the muscles starve.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.9' },
  { says: 'polyuria: glucose in the urine pulls water with it (osmotic diuresis)', why: 'The kidney filters glucose and normally reabsorbs almost all of it with sodium-glucose carriers in the proximal tubule. The carriers have a transport maximum — about 200 mg/dL of blood glucose. Above that, glucose stays in the urine and draws water with it by osmosis, so large volumes of urine are lost, the body dehydrates and thirst follows.', src: 'A&P 2e 25.6, 17.9' },
  { says: 'burning fat yields ketone bodies — acids', why: 'When cells can\'t use glucose, fat is broken down for fuel. Fatty acid breakdown makes acetyl CoA faster than the Krebs cycle can use it, and the liver diverts the excess into ketone bodies such as beta-hydroxybutyrate. Ketones are acids, and as they build up they cause diabetic ketoacidosis — a metabolic acidosis that can end in diabetic coma.', src: 'A&P 2e 24.3, 17.9, 26.5' },
  { says: 'Insulin, correcting acidosis and fluids drive potassium back into cells', why: 'A&P describes this directly: when insulin is given and glucose is taken up by cells, potassium passes into the cells with it, lowering potassium in the blood. Potassium sets the resting electrical charge of nerve and muscle cells, so a rapid fall weakens muscles and disturbs the heart rhythm — hence potassium checks every few hours during DKA treatment.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.3' }
],
'56-types-risk-and-screening': [
  { says: 'autoimmune destruction of beta cells → absolute insulin deficiency', why: 'Type 1 diabetes is a T-cell-dependent autoimmune disease: T cells, anti-beta-cell antibodies and macrophages selectively destroy the insulin-making beta cells, and viral infections may play a part in starting it. The destruction can take years, but symptoms — extreme thirst and urination, weight loss and fatigue — usually appear suddenly, because diagnosis comes only after most beta cells are gone.', src: 'Microbiology 19.2' },
  { says: 'insulin resistance first (cells respond poorly), then declining beta-cell secretion', why: 'In type 2 diabetes the body\'s cells become resistant to insulin. The pancreas answers by making more insulin, and over time the beta cells become exhausted. Diet, inactivity and prediabetes raise the risk, and moderate weight loss, regular activity and a healthy diet can reverse many cases — but if glucose can\'t be controlled, insulin is eventually needed.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.9' },
  { says: 'higher rates and burden in American Indian/Alaska Native', why: 'These numbers reflect health disparities: minority groups have higher rates of diabetes and related deaths, and even with the same insurance they receive poorer-quality care and less access to care. Because minority groups are also more likely to be poor, race and income barriers stack — which is why the notes ask for screening and advocacy at every visit.', src: 'Sociology 3e 19.3' }
],
'56-diagnosing-and-monitoring-glucose-a1c-cg': [
  { says: 'glucose binds hemoglobin (glycosylation)', why: 'Glucose attaches to hemoglobin inside red blood cells, and it stays attached for the life of the cell. A red blood cell lives about 120 days, so the share of glycated hemoglobin is an average of the last three to four months. A careful diet for a few days before the visit barely changes it.', src: 'A&P 2e 18.3' },
  { says: 'Interstitial glucose lags up to 30 minutes behind blood', why: 'A CGM sensor sits in the interstitial fluid — the fluid between cells — not in a blood vessel. Glucose must first leave the capillaries and diffuse into that fluid, so when blood glucose is changing fast, the interstitial reading trails behind. That is why a low or very high CGM number is confirmed with a fingerstick before treating it.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.1, 20.3' },
  { says: 'never share a meter or lancet holder (infection spreads even with a new lancet)', why: 'Bloodborne organisms can spread indirectly, through shared equipment: ineffective hand hygiene, improper cleaning of medical equipment and devices carried from one patient to another are all routes of infection. A trace of blood on a lancet holder or meter is enough, even if the lancet itself is new.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 10.1' }
],
'56-chronic-complications-vessels-eyes-nerve': [
  { says: 'chronic hyperglycemia thickens basement membranes, glucose is directly toxic to cells, and small-vessel ischemia causes tissue hypoxia', why: 'Persistently high glucose injures tissue throughout the body, especially blood vessels and nerves. Damaged artery linings lead to atherosclerosis, heart attack and stroke; damaged small vessels in the kidney and eye cause kidney failure and blindness; poor circulation to the limbs plus nerve damage (neuropathy) raises the risk of injury, infection and tissue death — the reason for so many toe, foot and leg amputations.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.9' },
  { says: 'Vascular changes plus high glucose weaken white cells, impair tissue oxygen and feed microbes', why: 'Diabetes harms wound healing three ways: it impairs the circulation that brings oxygen and nutrients, it damages nerves so a wound may not be felt, and uncontrolled glucose impairs the white blood cells that fight bacteria. Diabetes also produces a secondary immunodeficiency — the immune system works less well — so infections start more easily and spread faster.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 24.4 · Microbiology 19.4' }
],
'56-antidiabetic-drugs-non-insulin': [
  { says: 'the kidney can\'t reabsorb filtered glucose and sodium → they leave in urine', why: 'Glucose filtered by the kidney is normally almost 100% reabsorbed, by symport with sodium in the proximal tubule. SGLT2 inhibitors block that sodium-glucose carrier, so glucose and sodium stay in the urine and take water with them by osmosis. That is the drug\'s benefit and its risks in one: lower glucose, but also dehydration and low sodium, and sugary urine that feeds bacteria and yeast.', src: 'A&P 2e 25.6' },
  { says: 'release preformed insulin from beta cells — only work if some beta-cell function remains', why: 'In type 1 diabetes the beta cells have been destroyed and make no insulin, and in long-standing type 2 they become exhausted. A drug that squeezes insulin out of beta cells only works if there are working beta cells to squeeze — and because it releases insulin whether or not glucose is high, it can cause lows.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.9' },
  { says: 'slow carbohydrate digestion in the intestine → no after-meal spike', why: 'Only single sugars can be absorbed. Starch and table sugar must first be split by enzymes on the brush border of the small intestine (maltase, sucrase, alpha-dextrinase) into glucose and other monosaccharides. Blocking those enzymes slows the arrival of glucose in the blood. The undigested carbohydrate reaches the colon, where bacteria ferment it into hydrogen, carbon dioxide and methane — the bloating and flatulence these drugs cause.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.7, 23.5' }
],
'56-insulin-types-timing-injection-and-stora': [
  { says: 'Insulin is a small protein destroyed in the GI tract, so it is injected (or inhaled)', why: 'Protein drugs taken by mouth are quickly deactivated by enzymes in the stomach and duodenum — the same pepsin, trypsin and chymotrypsin that digest the protein in food. Injection (or inhalation, as with an inhaled insulin powder) puts insulin into the body without passing through the gut.', src: 'Nursing Pharmacology 2e 1.3 · A&P 2e 23.7' },
  { says: 'Heat, massage and exercising the injected limb speed absorption', why: 'A drug given into the skin or under it is absorbed only as fast as blood flow carries it away. Heat, rubbing and working the muscle all increase local blood flow, so insulin leaves the site faster and acts sooner than expected — the same reason heat and cold are kept off medication patches.', src: 'Nursing Pharmacology 2e 1.3' },
  { says: 'Never below 36°F (2.2°C) or above 86°F (30°C), never frozen', why: 'Insulin is a protein, and a protein works only while it holds its folded shape. Extreme heat, acids, bases and some other conditions denature proteins — they lose their shape and can no longer do their job, like milk curdling when lemon juice is added. Clumping, frosting or a change in clarity is visible evidence that the insulin has lost potency.', src: 'A&P 2e 2.5' }
],
'56-eating-and-exercise-with-diabetes': [
  { says: 'at least 25 g of fiber a day', why: 'Fiber is a carbohydrate the body\'s enzymes can\'t digest; bacteria in the large intestine break it down instead. Soluble fiber (fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes) helps control blood sugar by slowing the absorption of glucose after a meal, and insoluble fiber such as cellulose adds bulk to stool.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 21.1' },
  { says: 'without enough insulin (uncontrolled type 1), glucagon and epinephrine pour out liver glucose while cells can\'t use it', why: 'Exercise is a stress signal: epinephrine tells the liver and muscles to break glycogen into glucose, and falling glucose during exercise releases glucagon, which does the same and makes new glucose. With insulin present, working muscles take that glucose up. Without insulin, the glucose pours into the blood but can\'t get into the muscles, so glucose rises and fat is burned into ketones instead.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.6, 17.9' }
],
'56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule': [
  { says: 'so low glucose quickly becomes a brain problem', why: 'The organs, especially the brain, need a continuous supply of glucose. Brain cells take up glucose without insulin, straight from the blood, and in the body\'s survival priorities fuel for the brain comes first. When blood glucose drops, the brain has no reserve to fall back on — confusion, behavior change and seizures follow quickly.', src: 'A&P 2e 24.5, 17.9' },
  { says: 'adrenergic: shaky, pounding heart, nervous', why: 'A falling glucose is treated as an emergency: the sympathetic system releases epinephrine, which signals the liver to turn glycogen into glucose and at the same time raises the heart rate and blood pressure. The pounding heart and shakiness are side effects of that rescue — an early alarm that comes before the brain runs short.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.6' },
  { says: 'Beta blockers mask the warning signs', why: 'The racing heart and tremor of a low come from epinephrine acting on beta receptors, and a beta blocker sits on those receptors. Sweating is a different pathway — sweat glands answer acetylcholine, which is why atropine, an anticholinergic, stops sweating. So a patient on a beta blocker may notice sweating but miss the pounding heart.', src: 'Nursing Pharmacology 2e 4.2 · A&P 2e 17.6' },
  { says: 'Chocolate and fatty sweets absorb slowly — use glucose', why: 'Carbohydrate-heavy food empties from the stomach fastest; meals high in fat stay longest — up to 6 hours or more while the duodenum processes fatty chyme. Glucose itself is a single sugar that needs no digestion and is absorbed as soon as it reaches the intestine. Fat in chocolate delays all of that, which is why pure glucose comes first and a snack with protein comes after.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4, 23.7' },
  { says: 'Concentrated IV dextrose (e.g., D50) is hyperosmolar', why: 'A solution injected into the body should be isotonic with blood serum. A hypertonic solution pulls water out of cells by osmosis — they shrivel and can die (crenation). Concentrated dextrose that leaks out of the vein does exactly that to the surrounding tissue, which is why the IV is checked carefully before and during the push.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 11.4' }
],
'56-dka-and-hhs-the-hyperglycemic-emergencie': [
  { says: 'Kussmaul respirations', why: 'Ketones add acid to the blood. The lungs compensate within minutes: chemoreceptors sense the change and the breathing rate and depth increase to blow off CO2, because CO2 + H2O makes carbonic acid — breathing it out lowers the acid load. Kussmaul breathing is the body correcting a metabolic acidosis with its lungs.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.5, 26.4' },
  { says: 'D5 in 0.45% saline, to prevent hypoglycemia and cerebral edema from osmolarity falling too fast', why: 'Water moves by osmosis toward the side with more dissolved particles. In severe hyperglycemia the blood is highly concentrated, and brain cells adjust to it over time. If glucose (and with it osmolarity) is dropped too quickly, the blood becomes relatively dilute and water rushes into the brain cells, which swell. Adding dextrose once glucose reaches 250 slows that fall.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 11.4 · Fundamentals of Nursing 20.1' },
  { says: 'Enough insulin remains to prevent ketosis but not hyperglycemia', why: 'Ketones are made when cells can\'t use glucose and burn fat instead, sending more acetyl CoA to the liver than the Krebs cycle can handle. A small amount of insulin is enough to keep that fat-burning in check, so no ketoacidosis develops — but not enough to move all the glucose into cells, so it keeps climbing, often far higher than in DKA.', src: 'A&P 2e 24.3, 17.9' },
  { says: 'coma once osmolarity > 350', why: 'When the blood becomes very concentrated, water leaves cells by osmosis and they shrink. Brain cells are particularly sensitive to these fluid shifts, which is why rising osmolarity shows up as confusion, irritability, seizures and finally coma — and why the neurologic checks continue until the osmolarity is back to normal.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 20.1 · Chemistry 2e 11.4' },
  { says: 'blunted thirst, poor urine concentration and diuretics', why: 'Thirst is the body\'s main defense against concentrated blood: osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus trigger thirst and ADH. Some older adults develop a decreased sense of thirst, so the hyperglycemic diuresis of HHS can go on for days without the patient drinking enough to keep up — the reason HHS creeps up mostly on older adults.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.2 · Fundamentals of Nursing 41.3' }
],
'56-foot-care-kidneys-and-preventing-injury': [
  { says: 'Neuropathy is the main cause of ulcers; poor perfusion is the main cause of poor healing', why: 'Damaged nerves take away the warning: reduced sensation means the person may not know a wound is there, so care is delayed. Damaged vessels take away the repair: diabetes impairs the circulation that brings oxygen and nutrients, and oxygen is essential for building new vessels and collagen. Add white blood cells weakened by high glucose and a small blister can become an infected ulcer.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 24.4 · A&P 2e 17.9' }
],
'56-diabetes-in-the-hospital-and-around-surg': [
  { says: 'balancing hormones often raise glucose before a fever appears', why: 'Infection is a physical stressor, and stress hormones respond first: epinephrine tells the liver to turn glycogen into glucose, and cortisol breaks down glycogen, fat and protein to make more glucose. A rising glucose in a patient who was stable is the stress response showing up in the lab before a fever does — so the nurse looks for the infection.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.6' },
  { says: 'anesthesia and surgery release counterregulatory hormones', why: 'Surgery is a controlled injury, and the body reacts to it as it does to any stressor: the alarm reaction (epinephrine) and then cortisol through the HPA axis. Both raise blood glucose and work against insulin, so insulin needs rise around surgery even while the patient eats nothing.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.6 · Psychology 2e 14.1' }
],
'56-teaching-self-management': [
  { says: 'Break skills into small achievable tasks', why: 'Psychology shows that how stressful a demand feels depends largely on perceived control — our belief that we can influence the outcome — and that perceived control has major effects on health. Mastering one small skill at a time (drawing up an accurate dose first) builds that belief, which is what keeps someone injecting insulin at home every day.', src: 'Psychology 2e 14.4' },
  { says: 'hands-on practice, and teach-back', why: 'Even people with high general literacy may not understand health information, so teaching starts by checking literacy and health literacy and defining medical terms in plain language. Skills like injecting insulin are learned by doing (the psychomotor domain): the nurse demonstrates, the patient does a return demonstration, and teach-back confirms what was understood.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 17.2, 17.3' }
],
'45-overview': [
  { says: 'Parietal cells make hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor', why: 'The same parietal cells do two jobs. Their HCl makes stomach contents very acidic (pH 1.5–3.5), which turns pepsinogen into active pepsin, kills most swallowed bacteria and unfolds proteins for digestion. Their intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12, protects it from digestion and carries it to receptors in the terminal ileum, the only place B12 is absorbed. Lose the parietal cells and both acid and B12 absorption go.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4, 23.7' },
  { says: 'turns ammonia into urea, makes albumin, prothrombin and fibrinogen', why: 'Breaking down amino acids releases ammonia, which is toxic. The liver runs the urea cycle, turning ammonia into urea that the kidneys excrete. The liver also makes most clotting factors — and needs vitamin K, which gut bacteria help supply, to make many of them. A failing liver therefore shows high ammonia, low albumin and slow clotting.', src: 'A&P 2e 24.4, 18.5' },
  { says: 'Villi and circular folds make a huge surface', why: 'The small intestine has three features that multiply its absorbing surface more than 600-fold: circular folds that make chyme spiral and slow down, villi with a capillary bed and a lacteal in each, and microvilli (the brush border) carrying the enzymes that finish digestion. Anything that flattens them — like the inflammation of celiac disease — shrinks the surface and causes malabsorption.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' },
  { says: 'Slow segmental contractions allow water and electrolyte absorption, turning chyme into feces', why: 'The small intestine absorbs about 90% of the water that passes through it; the colon absorbs most of the rest while residue lingers there for 12 to 24 hours, mixed by slow haustral contractions about every 30 minutes. Of about 500 mL of residue entering the cecum each day, only about 150 mL becomes feces. Slow transit makes firmer stool; fast transit leaves it watery.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' }
],
'45-gi-changes-with-aging': [
  { says: 'gastric mucosa atrophies → less hydrochloric acid (hypochlorhydria)', why: 'Stomach acid kills much of the bacteria swallowed with food, so less acid lets more bacteria survive and overgrow. The acid-making parietal cells also make intrinsic factor, which vitamin B12 needs to be absorbed. And changes in gastric pH with age can alter the absorption of certain medications.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4 · Nursing Pharmacology 2e 1.3' },
  { says: 'less urge to defecate → postponed bowel movements → constipation and impaction', why: 'The urge to defecate is a reflex: mass movements stretch the rectum and trigger the defecation reflex. If the urge is ignored, the reflex fades and the colon keeps absorbing water from the waiting stool, making it firmer — the start of constipation and, if it continues, impaction.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' },
  { says: 'drugs metabolized slowly and can build up to toxic levels', why: 'The liver breaks most drugs down (mainly with cytochrome P450 enzymes), and liver metabolism may significantly decline with age. First-pass metabolism also decreases, so more of each dose reaches the circulation as free drug. The result is higher drug levels and a longer half-life — why older adults often need lower doses.', src: 'Nursing Pharmacology 2e 1.5' }
],
'45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam': [
  { says: 'lactose intolerance (about 50% of US adults acquire it', why: 'Lactose (milk sugar) must be split by lactase, an enzyme on the brush border of the small intestine. Without enough lactase it passes undigested into the colon, where bacteria ferment it, producing gas, cramps, bloating and diarrhea. The hydrogen made by that fermentation is absorbed and breathed out, which is what a hydrogen breath test measures.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5, 23.7' }
],
'45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests': [
  { says: 'so liver disease prolongs PT', why: 'The clotting factors circulate as proteins made mostly by the liver, and the liver needs fat-soluble vitamin K to produce many of them. Vitamin K comes from the diet and from bacteria in the large intestine. A damaged liver (or poor fat absorption, which limits vitamin K) means fewer factors, so the blood takes longer to clot.', src: 'A&P 2e 18.5' },
  { says: 'indirect (unconjugated) 0.2–0.8 with hemolysis', why: 'Bilirubin is a waste product of old red blood cells: heme is broken down to bilirubin, which rides to the liver on albumin. The liver processes it and excretes it in bile, and gut bacteria turn it into stercobilin, the brown of normal stool. Too many red cells breaking down raises the bilirubin arriving at the liver (indirect); bile that can\'t drain backs processed bilirubin up into the blood (direct) — and stool turns pale.', src: 'A&P 2e 18.3, 23.6' },
  { says: 'rise in acute pancreatitis', why: 'The pancreas makes its protein-digesting enzymes in an inactive form, to be switched on only in the duodenum — if they were active inside the gland they would digest the pancreas, which is exactly what happens in pancreatitis. Injured pancreatic cells leak their enzymes, including amylase (starch) and lipase (fat), into the blood, so their levels rise.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.6, 23.7' },
  { says: 'fecal fat when steatorrhea or malabsorption is suspected', why: 'Fat needs two things to be absorbed: bile salts, which break big fat globules into tiny droplets the way dish soap does, and pancreatic lipase, which digests them. When bile can\'t reach the intestine the stool turns white and high in fat, because almost no fat is broken down or absorbed. Measuring fat in stool tests that whole system.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.6' }
],
'45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule': [
  { says: 'a local anesthetic spray numbs the gag reflex', why: 'Swallowing is protected by reflexes: when food reaches the pharynx, involuntary muscle contractions close the airway — the soft palate rises and the epiglottis folds down over the larynx — and anything that goes down the wrong way triggers a cough. Numbing the throat switches off that sensing, so food or fluid can slip into the airway unnoticed until the gag reflex returns.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.3' }
],
'45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal': [
  { says: 'Bowel prep starts the night before', why: 'The aim of a bowel prep is a colon empty of stool so the lining can be seen. The preparation solutions work like osmosis in a tube: a large amount of fluid carrying dissolved substance the gut doesn\'t absorb stays in the bowel and flushes it out. Water and electrolytes leave with it, which is why the prep comes with electrolyte drinks and close attention to fluids.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 27.3 · Chemistry 2e 11.4' },
  { says: 'people vary widely in finding, understanding and using health information', why: 'Literacy and health literacy are separate: people with high reading levels may still not understand health information. That is why teaching starts by assessing health literacy, uses plain language, and ends with teach-back and demonstration to check what was actually understood — a missed prep step means a missed screening.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 17.2, 17.3' }
],
'46-overview': [
  { says: 'the lower esophageal sphincter keeps a higher pressure than the stomach', why: 'The lower esophageal sphincter relaxes to let food into the stomach and then contracts to keep stomach acid from backing up into the esophagus; the muscular diaphragm around it helps close it when nothing is being swallowed. When the sphincter doesn\'t close completely, stomach contents reflux, causing heartburn or GERD.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.3' },
  { says: 'columnar cells that resist acid but are premalignant', why: 'The esophagus is lined with stratified squamous epithelium, built in layers to resist wear and tear; the stomach and intestines are lined with simple columnar epithelium with mucus-secreting goblet cells. Barrett\'s esophagus is the esophagus swapping its normal lining for the stomach-style columnar type under constant acid — better at resisting acid, but abnormal tissue that can become cancer.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.1, 4.2' },
  { says: 'the esophagus has no serosa to contain a tumor', why: 'Most of the digestive tract is wrapped in serosa — visceral peritoneum — but the mouth, pharynx and esophagus have only an adventitia, a sheath of collagen fibers. With no serosal layer to hold it back, an esophageal tumor can grow through the wall into nearby tissue, which is part of why these cancers are often advanced at diagnosis.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.1' }
],
'46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar': [
  { says: 'overgrows when long-term antibiotics destroy normal flora', why: 'Candida is part of the normal mouth flora, kept in check by the bacteria living alongside it and by the immune system. Antibiotics kill those bacteria, and infants, immunodeficient patients and people on chemotherapy lack the defenses, so Candida overgrows into thrush: white patches and pseudomembranes, treated with nystatin or clotrimazole.', src: 'Microbiology 24.2, 24.1' },
  { says: 'Secondary: opportunistic viruses, fungi or bacteria in people who are immunocompromised or on chemotherapy, radiation or steroids', why: 'Radiation and many chemotherapy drugs damage cells that divide rapidly — the lining of the GI tract renews constantly — so the mouth breaks down and heals slowly. Chemotherapy and radiation also suppress the bone marrow and immune system, and steroids damp the inflammatory response, so normally harmless organisms like Candida and herpes simplex can take hold.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 21.6 · Microbiology 19.4 · Fundamentals of Nursing 24.4' }
],
'46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla': [
  { says: 'Treat the cause and increase saliva flow', why: 'Saliva is a defense system, not just moisture. Bicarbonate and phosphate ions buffer it to near neutral, IgA keeps microbes from penetrating the lining, lysozyme kills bacteria, and its mucus lets food form a bolus that can be swallowed. Without saliva, bacteria thrive — they can travel up the ducts into the glands, and teeth decay.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.3' }
],
'46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar': [
  { says: 'overweight and obesity (raised abdominal pressure) are the biggest', why: 'Whatever raises pressure inside the abdomen pushes on the stomach from outside — the same pressure we create on purpose when we strain (the Valsalva maneuver tightens the diaphragm and abdominal wall). A sphincter that is barely holding is overpowered, and stomach contents move up into the esophagus.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.3, 23.5' },
  { says: 'Ambulatory esophageal pH monitoring is the most accurate', why: 'Stomach contents are strongly acidic (pH 1.5–3.5), while the esophagus normally isn\'t. pH is a logarithmic scale — each whole number is a tenfold change in acid — so a probe in the lower esophagus can clearly record each time acid washes up, how long it stays and whether it matches the symptoms.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4 · Chemistry 2e 14.2' }
],
'46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce': [
  { says: '4–6 small meals instead of 3 large ones', why: 'The stomach can stretch to hold about 4 L, and it empties a little at a time — about 3 mL of chyme per push through the pylorus. A big meal stretches it and stays longer; fatty food is the slowest to leave (6 hours or more), carbohydrate the fastest. Smaller, lower-fat meals leave less in the stomach, for less time, to push against the sphincter.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4' },
  { says: 'antacids, histamine (H₂) blockers and proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)', why: 'The three classes act at three points. Antacids neutralize acid already made — for example, magnesium hydroxide + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + water. H2 blockers stop one signal for acid: histamine, which stimulates the parietal cells to release HCl. PPIs shut down the acid-making step in the parietal cell itself, whatever the signal.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 4.2 · A&P 2e 23.4' }
],
'46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care': [
  { says: 'the stomach pushes through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm into the chest', why: 'The esophagus passes into the abdomen through an opening in the diaphragm, the esophageal hiatus, and the diaphragm\'s muscle around the lower esophageal sphincter helps close it. When the junction slides up into the chest, it loses that extra squeeze — which is why a sliding hiatal hernia mainly causes reflux and is treated like GERD.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.3' }
],
'47-overview': [
  { says: 'prostaglandins maintain the protective barrier', why: 'The stomach should digest itself — its enzymes digest protein and its acid is corrosive. It doesn\'t because of the mucosal barrier: a thick layer of bicarbonate-rich mucus that neutralizes acid, tight junctions between lining cells, and stem cells that replace the whole surface lining every 3 to 6 days. NSAIDs (including aspirin) and H. pylori break that barrier, and together they cause most ulcers.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4' },
  { says: 'Its urease turns urea into ammonia, which neutralizes the acid around it', why: 'H. pylori clings to stomach cells with pili and makes urease; the ammonia it produces neutralizes acid around the bacterium. The infection damages the cells that make protective mucus, causing gastritis and, slowly, ulcers. About two-thirds of people carry it, but fewer than 20% develop ulcers or stomach cancer — yet it is found in about 80% of stomach ulcers and over 90% of duodenal ulcers.', src: 'Microbiology 24.3' },
  { says: 'intrinsic factor is lost', why: 'Intrinsic factor, made by the same parietal cells that make acid, binds vitamin B12 and carries it to the terminal ileum to be absorbed. B12 and folate are coenzymes for DNA synthesis, which red blood cell production depends on — so without intrinsic factor, B12 stores run down and anemia follows.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4, 23.7, 18.3' }
],
'47-gastritis': [
  { says: 'long-term NSAIDs (the big one)', why: 'NSAIDs relieve pain by blocking prostaglandin production — but they block it everywhere, including in the stomach lining, where prostaglandins help maintain the protective barrier. That is why the majority of ulcers are caused by either NSAIDs (including aspirin) or H. pylori, and why switching pain relievers lets an NSAID ulcer heal.', src: 'A&P 2e 21.2, 23.4' },
  { says: 'Results are more reliable if antacids and PPIs are stopped for at least a week before', why: 'H. pylori tests look for the bacterium or its urease activity. H. pylori prefers an acidic environment, and acid suppression is actually part of the treatment for that reason — so a PPI taken before the test can reduce the bacteria enough to give a false negative.', src: 'Microbiology 24.3' }
],
'47-ulcer-and-gastritis-drugs-table-47-1': [
  { says: 'raise gastric pH and deactivate pepsin', why: 'An antacid is a base, and a base plus an acid gives a salt and water: magnesium hydroxide + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + water. Less acid means a higher pH, and pepsin needs acid — HCl converts pepsinogen into active pepsin — so neutralizing the acid also quiets the protein-digesting enzyme.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 4.2 · A&P 2e 23.4' },
  { says: 'block histamine receptors on parietal cells', why: 'Several signals make parietal cells secrete HCl: acetylcholine from the vagus nerve, gastrin, and histamine. Histamine acts on H2 receptors on the parietal cells, and blocking those receptors removes one of the main drivers of acid — which is why one bedtime dose can quiet night-time acid.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4' },
  { says: 'No single drug eradicates H. pylori', why: 'Unique among bacterial infections, H. pylori is treated with a combination: a 10–14 day regimen such as omeprazole + amoxicillin + clarithromycin, or bismuth + metronidazole + tetracycline. The acid reducers aren\'t antibiotics — they lower acid because H. pylori prefers acidic conditions. Combining drugs also makes resistance less likely to emerge.', src: 'Microbiology 24.3, 14.5' },
  { says: 'sunscreen (photosensitivity)', why: 'Tetracyclines stop bacteria from making proteins by blocking the ribosome. They are broad-spectrum, but phototoxicity is a known side effect that can limit their use — the skin burns more easily in sunlight — so patients are taught to use sunscreen and limit sun exposure while taking them.', src: 'Microbiology 14.3' }
],
'47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp': [
  { says: 'Food buffers acid for 30–60 minutes, then acid rebounds', why: 'Food itself turns on acid secretion. In the gastric phase, which lasts 3 to 4 hours, stretch of the stomach, partly digested proteins, caffeine and the rising pH from buffering all release gastrin, which makes parietal cells produce more HCl. So the relief from eating is short and followed by more acid — the reason diet doesn\'t heal ulcers and bedtime snacks are discouraged.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4' },
  { says: 'the urea breath test', why: 'In the breath test the patient swallows labeled urea. If H. pylori is present, its urease splits the urea and the labeled carbon comes out as carbon dioxide in the breath. No urease, no labeled CO2 — a simple chemical signature of a live infection.', src: 'Microbiology 24.3' },
  { says: 'coffee-ground when acid has digested the blood', why: 'Hemoglobin is a protein, and stomach acid denatures proteins while pepsin begins digesting them. Blood that stays in the stomach long enough is broken down into dark granules, while blood that is vomited quickly is still bright red. Black tarry stools (melena) suggest bleeding high in the GI tract; bright red blood in stool suggests lower GI bleeding.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4 · Fundamentals of Nursing 27.3' },
  { says: 'metabolic alkalosis and hypokalemia', why: 'Vomited stomach juice is hydrochloric acid, and losing acid leaves the blood with relatively too much bicarbonate — metabolic alkalosis. Potassium is lost as well, both in the vomitus and because alkalosis itself lowers blood potassium. That is why prolonged vomiting or NG suction needs electrolyte checks and replacement.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.5, 26.3' },
  { says: 'emotional stress worsens GI disorders through the gut-brain axis', why: 'When Selye exposed rats to prolonged stressors, among the first signs he found were enlarged adrenal glands, shrunken lymph tissue and stomach ulceration. Stress triggers sympathetic and HPA (cortisol) responses that affect almost every organ, and disorders whose symptoms are produced or worsened by stress are called psychophysiological — real physical disease, made worse by the mind.', src: 'Psychology 2e 14.1, 14.3' }
],
'47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies': [
  { says: 'start isotonic fluid at once', why: 'Fluids given into a vein must be isotonic — the same concentration of dissolved particles as blood serum — so water stays in the blood vessels instead of moving into or out of cells. A hypotonic fluid would swell and burst red cells; a hypertonic one would shrink them. Normal saline and lactated Ringer\'s replace lost volume without that shift.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 11.4' }
],
'47-gastric-cancer-and-life-after-gastrectom': [
  { says: 'as food rushes into the small intestine and pulls fluid into the gut', why: 'Normally the pylorus lets only about 3 mL of chyme into the duodenum at a time, because chyme is hypertonic. A&P spells out what happens otherwise: if large amounts were forced into the small intestine at once, osmosis would pull water from the blood into the intestine — a potentially dangerous drop in blood volume. After gastric surgery that brake is gone, and early dumping is exactly that.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5, 23.4' },
  { says: 'carbohydrate enters the jejunum fast', why: 'Food in the intestine triggers gut hormones that start insulin release, and the surge of glucose after absorption drives insulin higher still. When a meal\'s sugar is absorbed all at once, the insulin response overshoots, and an hour or two later glucose drops below normal — late dumping is a reactive hypoglycemia.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.9' },
  { says: 'high-protein, high-fat, low- to moderate-carbohydrate diet', why: 'Foods leave the stomach and are absorbed at different speeds: carbohydrate-heavy food is fastest, protein next, and fatty meals stay longest because fat is digested slowly. A meal built on protein and fat moves through more slowly and releases glucose gradually, so it pulls less water into the bowel and provokes less of an insulin spike.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4' },
  { says: 'Vitamin B₁₂, folic acid and iron deficiency', why: 'Removing the stomach removes the parietal cells, and with them intrinsic factor — without it vitamin B12 can\'t be absorbed in the ileum. B12 and folate are needed for DNA synthesis in new red blood cells, so their lack causes anemia. Losing stomach acid also changes how some minerals and drugs are absorbed.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4, 23.7, 18.3' }
],
'48-overview': [
  { says: 'High small-bowel block → loss of gastric hydrochloric acid → metabolic alkalosis', why: 'Vomiting stomach contents loses hydrochloric acid, leaving too much bicarbonate in the blood — metabolic alkalosis. Losing intestinal fluid from lower down (as in diarrhea) loses bicarbonate instead — metabolic acidosis. The site of the fluid loss decides which way the pH goes.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.5' },
  { says: 'stagnant bacteria in a blocked loop are harmless until blood flow fails', why: 'Trillions of bacteria live in the large intestine, and most are harmless as long as they stay in the gut lumen; the mucosal barrier and the immune cells within it keep them out of the body. When the bowel wall dies from lost blood supply, that barrier fails, and the same bacteria and their toxins reach the peritoneum and blood.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' }
],
'48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e': [
  { says: 'hypokalemia predisposes to it', why: 'Potassium sets the resting electrical charge of nerve and muscle cells. When blood potassium is low, cell membranes become hyperpolarized and respond less to stimuli — including the smooth muscle of the bowel. A gut that responds poorly to its signals slows down, which is why a low potassium can cause or prolong an ileus.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.3' },
  { says: 'Hgb, Hct and BUN rise (dehydration)', why: 'When water is lost from the blood, everything dissolved or suspended in it becomes more concentrated — hemoconcentration. No red cells were added and no extra urea was made; the same amounts are simply in less water. That is why these "high" values come back down as fluids are replaced.', src: 'A&P 2e 26.3' }
],
'48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid': [
  { says: 'IV isotonic fluid (NS or LR), often 2–4 L, with potassium', why: 'Replacement fluid must be isotonic so it stays in the circulation without shifting water into or out of cells. Potassium is added because vomiting and suction remove it, and low potassium weakens the very bowel muscle that needs to start moving again.', src: 'Chemistry 2e 11.4 · A&P 2e 26.3' }
],
'48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia': [
  { says: 'long-term smoking, obesity, inactivity, heavy alcohol', why: 'Colorectal cancer is one of the deadliest cancers, and A&P lists the same risks: a family history, smoking, excessive alcohol and a diet high in animal fat and protein. Several of these are habits, which is why prevention teaching sits next to screening.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' }
],
'48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and': [
  { says: 'Stool consistency follows location: ascending → liquid, transverse → pasty, descending → formed', why: 'Residue enters the colon still watery, and the colon pulls water out of it along its whole length while it lingers there for 12 to 24 hours. A stoma on the ascending colon receives stool before much water has been removed; one on the descending or sigmoid colon receives stool that has had nearly the whole colon to dry out.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' },
  { says: 'never an air ring or rubber donut', why: 'Ring cushions (donuts) should not be used because they can cause increased venous pressure in the surrounding areas — the ring presses on the tissue around the wound and impairs its circulation, when blood flow is exactly what a healing perineal wound needs. Foam that spreads the weight is safer.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 24.4' },
  { says: 'grief over body image', why: 'Changes in appearance from illness or treatment can upend body image and self-esteem, with feelings of shame, embarrassment or even disgust toward a body that no longer matches the person\'s sense of self. That is why feelings about the stoma and about sexuality are raised before surgery and revisited after.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 32.2' }
],
'48-irritable-bowel-syndrome': [
  { says: 'Hydrogen breath test: bacterial overgrowth or malabsorption makes extra hydrogen, which is exhaled', why: 'Humans can\'t digest some sugars (such as lactulose, the test drink), but bacteria can: their fermentation produces hydrogen, carbon dioxide and methane. Some of the hydrogen is absorbed into the blood and carried to the lungs, where it is breathed out. More bacteria in the wrong place, or more undigested sugar reaching them, means more hydrogen in the breath.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' },
  { says: 'Strong links to anxiety and depression', why: 'Psychology lists irritable bowel syndrome as a psychophysiological disorder: a physical disease whose symptoms are real and can be produced or worsened by stress and emotional factors. That is why stress reduction, counseling and relaxation are part of IBS treatment alongside fiber and drugs.', src: 'Psychology 2e 14.3' }
],
'48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids': [
  { says: 'often after straining or lifting', why: 'Straining is the Valsalva maneuver: contracting the diaphragm and abdominal muscles with the glottis closed to raise the pressure inside the abdomen. That pressure pushes outward on every weak spot in the abdominal wall — which is why hernias appear after lifting or straining, and why coughing and straining are avoided after a repair.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' },
  { says: 'Internal (above the sphincter, not visible) or external (below, visible)', why: 'The anal canal has a dividing line (the pectinate line). The lining above it is supplied by visceral sensory nerves and is fairly insensitive; the area below has somatic nerves like skin and is very sensitive. That is why hemorrhoids above the line often bleed without much pain, while those below it hurt.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' }
],
'49-overview': [
  { says: 'inflammation dilates vessels and makes them leaky so white cells can reach the bacteria', why: 'Mediators such as histamine dilate local vessels (heat and redness) and make capillaries leaky, so plasma escapes into the tissue (swelling); prostaglandins add vasodilation and pain. The leak lets neutrophils, clotting factors and other defenses reach the site. In a small area this walls an infection off — across the whole peritoneum, the same leak drains the circulation.', src: 'A&P 2e 21.2' },
  { says: 'a low-fiber diet makes small, hard stools', why: 'Insoluble fiber such as cellulose isn\'t digested and acts as a bulking agent for stool. Without it, stool is small and hard; the colon keeps pulling water from slow-moving stool, and passing it takes more straining. Higher pressure inside the colon pushes its lining out at weak points — diverticula.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 21.1 · A&P 2e 23.5' }
],
'49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar': [
  { says: 'common bacteria: Escherichia coli, Streptococcus, Staphylococcus, pneumococci, gonococci', why: 'Most of the bacteria in the large intestine are harmless commensals as long as they stay in the gut lumen — an immune system in the gut wall keeps them from crossing the mucosal barrier. When the bowel or appendix perforates, those same organisms land in the normally sterile peritoneum, where they cause infection.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5 · Microbiology 24.1' },
  { says: 'Chemical peritonitis comes from leaking bile, pancreatic enzymes or gastric acid', why: 'Digestive juices are built to dismantle food: stomach enzymes digest protein — the stomach survives only behind its mucosal barrier — and pancreatic enzymes are made inactive because, active, they would digest the pancreas itself. The peritoneum has no such protection, so leaking acid, enzymes or bile injure it chemically even before bacteria arrive.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.4, 23.6' }
],
'49-appendicitis': [
  { says: 'usually by a fecalith', why: 'The appendix is a narrow, twisted tube off the cecum with lymphoid tissue in its wall, and its shape makes it a haven where enteric bacteria collect and multiply. Block the opening and the trapped bacteria multiply in a closed space with rising pressure and failing blood flow — the setup for infection, gangrene and perforation.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' },
  { says: 'WBC is often 10,000–18,000/mm³', why: 'Neutrophils are normally 50–70% of white cells and are the rapid responders to bacterial infection. When the demand is high, the marrow releases younger, immature neutrophils called bands. More bands on the differential is what a shift to the left means: the marrow is releasing young cells to fight a bacterial infection.', src: 'A&P 2e 18.4' }
],
'49-gastroenteritis': [
  { says: 'the leading foodborne cause of gastroenteritis', why: 'Noroviruses are easily transmitted and highly contagious, known for outbreaks in confined groups such as cruise ships. They spread by direct contact, contaminated surfaces and food, and they are not killed by disinfectants at the standard concentrations used for bacteria — so the risk of spread stays high even after cleaning. Illness starts 12–48 hours after exposure, sometimes with projectile vomiting.', src: 'Microbiology 24.4' },
  { says: 'Oral rehydration therapy (ORT) such as sports drinks or Pedialyte replaces fluids and electrolytes', why: 'In the small intestine glucose is absorbed by co-transport with sodium, and water follows the absorbed solutes by osmosis. A drink containing both sugar and salt uses that carrier to pull water back into the body even during diarrhea, which is why oral rehydration with electrolyte solutions is the main treatment for most GI infections.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.7 · Microbiology 24.3' },
  { says: 'wash hands for at least 30 seconds with soap', why: 'GI infections spread by the fecal-oral route, often indirectly through hands, surfaces and shared objects. Norovirus resists standard disinfectants, and organisms on hands are physically removed by thorough washing with soap — which is why handwashing, not hand sanitizer alone, is the core of the teaching.', src: 'Microbiology 24.4 · Fundamentals of Nursing 10.1' }
],
'49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem': [
  { says: 'antibiotics in the past 2–3 months', why: 'Clostridioides difficile can live quietly as part of the normal gut flora. Long-term antibiotics disrupt the other bacteria that keep it in check, and C. difficile overgrows; its toxins kill colon cells and can cause pseudomembranous colitis with watery diarrhea, fever and pain. PPIs and hospital stays add risk, and its spores survive for long periods on surfaces.', src: 'Microbiology 24.3' },
  { says: 'CRP rises in the first 6 hours of inflammation', why: 'C-reactive protein is made by the liver early in an infection or inflammation. It binds to the walls of bacteria so that phagocytes such as macrophages can recognize and engulf them. Because the liver makes it quickly, CRP is a sensitive, early marker that inflammation is active.', src: 'A&P 2e 21.2' }
],
'49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a': [
  { says: 'It is related to sulfonamide antibiotics', why: 'The sulfonamides were the first synthetic antibacterial drugs; they block bacteria from making folic acid. A drug allergy is a type of hypersensitivity, and a person sensitized to one sulfa drug can react to a related one — which is why sulfa allergy is checked before sulfasalazine is started.', src: 'Microbiology 14.3, 19.1' },
  { says: 'Give a folic acid supplement — it decreases folate absorption', why: 'Folate and vitamin B12 are coenzymes for DNA synthesis, so they are critical for making new cells, including red blood cells. Too little of either causes a vitamin-deficient anemia — a risk already high in a patient losing blood from an inflamed colon.', src: 'A&P 2e 18.3' },
  { says: 'then tapered after improvement', why: 'A glucocorticoid drug feeds into the same negative feedback loop as natural cortisol: high levels tell the hypothalamus and pituitary to stop signaling the adrenal glands. During a long course the adrenal cortex is idle, so the dose is lowered gradually to let it resume work — stopping suddenly can leave the body short of cortisol.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.2, 17.6' },
  { says: 'They suppress immunity', why: 'Anything that weakens the immune response — a disease such as diabetes or malnutrition, or an immunosuppressive treatment — produces a secondary immunodeficiency. Immunosuppression leaves a person open to infections that a healthy immune system would stop, which is why patients on biologics and steroids avoid crowds and report even a cold.', src: 'Microbiology 19.4 · Psychology 2e 14.3' },
  { says: 'bright red (frank) or black and tarry (melena)', why: 'Stool color shows where blood came from. Black, sticky, tarry stool (melena) suggests bleeding high in the GI tract, because the blood has been digested on the way down; bright red blood in the stool (hematochezia) is a sign of bleeding lower down, close to the exit — the usual pattern in ulcerative colitis.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 27.3' }
],
'49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou': [
  { says: 'the small bowel absorbs more sodium and water', why: 'The colon\'s job is to finish absorbing water and salts, and A&P notes the large intestine can be completely removed without much effect on digestion — the small intestine already absorbs about 90% of water and 80% of electrolytes. After a colectomy it absorbs more still, so the ileostomy output thickens over time.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5, 23.7' },
  { says: 'Small-intestine stool is full of enzymes and bile salts that quickly excoriate skin', why: 'Pancreatic juice brings protein-digesting enzymes (trypsin, chymotrypsin) and lipase into the small intestine, and bile salts break up fats like detergent. Normally the colon dilutes and dries these out; from an ileostomy they reach the skin still active, and skin is protein and fat — so a good barrier seal is a priority.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.6, 23.7' },
  { says: 'Vitamin B12 may be needed if the terminal ileum is removed', why: 'Vitamin B12 is too large a molecule to be absorbed by simple diffusion. Intrinsic factor from the stomach binds it and carries it to receptors found only in the terminal ileum, where it is taken up. Remove or bypass the terminal ileum and B12 can no longer be absorbed by mouth.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.7' },
  { says: 'avoid enteric-coated and capsule medicines', why: 'How a pill is absorbed depends on its coating, gastric emptying and the time it spends in the gut. Enteric coatings and extended-release forms are designed to dissolve slowly along a full-length intestine; with a shortened bowel and fast transit, they may come out in the pouch before releasing the drug.', src: 'Nursing Pharmacology 2e 1.3' }
],
'49-crohn-s-disease': [
  { says: 'steatorrhea (fatty stools) is common', why: 'Fat absorption needs bile salts, and the body recycles them: once bile salts reach the ileum, they are absorbed and returned to the liver to be used again (the enterohepatic circulation). Crohn\'s disease favors the terminal ileum, so bile salts are lost in stool instead, too few are left to emulsify fat, and undigested fat passes through.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.6, 23.7' },
  { says: 'monthly B12 injections', why: 'Bile salts and vitamin B12 are both absorbed in the terminal ileum — B12 only after it has been bound by intrinsic factor. When the terminal ileum is inflamed, scarred or removed, swallowed B12 can\'t be absorbed, so it has to be given by injection, bypassing the gut entirely.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.7' },
  { says: 'they mask infection', why: 'Glucocorticoids downregulate the immune system and blunt the inflammatory phase — that is why they calm a flare. The same effect hides the usual signs of infection (fever, redness, pain), so an abscess or fistula infection can progress quietly. Short courses and close monitoring limit the risk.', src: 'A&P 2e 17.6 · Fundamentals of Nursing 24.4' },
  { says: 'intestinal enzymes are caustic', why: 'Pancreatic enzymes are kept inactive until they reach the duodenum because, active, they digest proteins and fats indiscriminately — the pancreas itself would be digested otherwise. Fistula drainage carries these active enzymes, and skin is made of protein and fat, so drainage on skin breaks it down fast.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.6' }
],
'49-diverticular-disease': [
  { says: '25–35 g a day', why: 'Insoluble fiber such as cellulose isn\'t digested; it adds bulk to stool, and soluble fiber holds water. Bulky, soft stool moves through the colon with less squeezing and less straining, which keeps pressure inside the colon low — the pressure that pushes the lining out through weak spots to form diverticula. Fiber is added gradually because colon bacteria ferment it into gas.', src: 'Fundamentals of Nursing 21.1 · A&P 2e 23.5' },
  { says: 'no lifting, straining, coughing or bending', why: 'Lifting, coughing and straining all raise intra-abdominal pressure, the way the Valsalva maneuver does — contracting the diaphragm and abdominal muscles against a closed glottis. That pressure is transmitted to the colon, and an inflamed diverticulum is a thin, weakened pouch that can rupture under it.', src: 'A&P 2e 23.5' }
],
'49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections': [
  { says: 'chronic inflammation of the small-intestine lining that can cause bowel wall atrophy, malabsorption and diarrhea', why: 'Celiac disease is an organ-specific autoimmune disease: gluten (in wheat, barley and rye) triggers autoantibodies and inflammation that reduce the depth of the microvilli. The microvilli, villi and folds give the small intestine more than 600 times the surface it would otherwise have, so flattening them sharply cuts absorption — weight loss and anemia follow.', src: 'Microbiology 19.2 · A&P 2e 23.5' },
  { says: 'screening blood test and endoscopy', why: 'Serology looks for IgA antibodies against components of gluten and against the transglutaminase enzyme and endomysium; positive results are followed by endoscopy with a biopsy of the duodenum to see the damage. Screening can find people with no symptoms yet.', src: 'Microbiology 19.2' },
  { says: 'shared water supply', why: 'Giardia has a resistant cyst stage that survives cold and the chlorination used for municipal drinking water, so water must be filtered to remove the cysts. Cryptosporidium outbreaks come from contaminated public water, water parks and swimming pools. When several people from one household or group get sick, the shared water is the first suspect.', src: 'Microbiology 24.5' },
  { says: 'metronidazole followed by a luminal agent such as paromomycin', why: 'Metronidazole interferes with DNA replication in the organism and is first-line for amebiasis and giardiasis. It works on the invading parasites, but cysts can remain in the bowel; a second drug such as paromomycin follows to eliminate the remaining cysts, which is why therapy is checked with repeated stool exams.', src: 'Microbiology 24.5, 14.3' }
]
};
